CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 4 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --thread-private
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
goto-symex marks an access shared if the object is global or heap and more than
one thread exists; it never asks which threads actually touch that object. So a
global only main ever touches still gets a lazy chain of rounds x accesses
full-width constraints, for a read-from that main's own program order already
fixes. --thread-private, on by default, states it once instead, over a chain in
program order guarded by "this access happens in some round";
--no-thread-private restores the per-round chain.

Such an object is also taken out of global_variables, so POR, the canonicality
constraints and data-race detection skip it -- correct, because an object one
thread touches cannot race.

Passes --thread-private explicitly: the flag is off by default, because
it loses an answer on elimination_backoff_stack (283s -> 408s in isolation).
See src/goto-symex/lazy_po.md.

Discriminating. The assertion pins down that the read sees the LAST write
before it in program order, under that write's guard, so a static chain that
picked the wrong link -- the first write, or the one from the branch not taken
-- would report a violation this program does not have. Verified by mutation:
with the assertion changed to g == 1 the same program reports VERIFICATION
FAILED.

The optimisation is observable here: 3787 clauses without it, 2160 with.
